### Essay: «Why Reading Books Is Important»

Reading books is one of the most valuable habits a person can develop. In today’s world, where digital entertainment dominates, many teenagers prefer watching videos or playing games instead of reading. However, I strongly believe that books remain essential for personal growth and development.

First of all, reading expands our vocabulary and improves language skills. When we read, we encounter new words and phrases that we might not hear in everyday conversations. This helps us speak and write more fluently. For example, after reading classic novels, I noticed that my essays at school became more interesting and detailed.

Secondly, books develop our imagination. Unlike films or videos, books don’t show us ready‑made images — they let us create them in our minds. When I read fantasy stories, I can picture magical worlds and characters as I imagine them. This creative thinking is useful not only in literature but in many other areas of life.

Moreover, reading teaches us empathy. By following the characters’ journeys, we learn to understand different perspectives and emotions. For instance, when I read about a character facing difficulties, I start thinking about how I would react in a similar situation. This helps me better understand my friends and family.

Another important benefit is that books provide knowledge. Non‑fiction books teach us about history, science, and culture. Even fiction often contains useful information about human nature and society. Regular reading makes us more informed and helps us make better decisions.

Finally, reading is a great way to relax and reduce stress. In just 15–20 minutes a day, we can escape from daily worries and immerse ourselves in another world. This quiet time helps me recharge and feel more focused.

In conclusion, reading books is not just a hobby — it’s an investment in ourselves. It improves our language, develops imagination, teaches empathy, provides knowledge, and helps us relax. I encourage everyone to make reading a regular part of their lives, as it truly changes us for the better.

